1
00:00:00,281 --> 00:00:03,488
Two identical steel balls sit side by side

2
00:00:03,648 --> 00:00:04,590
on the edge of a bench,

3
00:00:05,212 --> 00:00:06,956
one point two metres above the floor.

4
00:00:07,697 --> 00:00:10,822
When this spring-loaded rod is released, it knocks

5
00:00:10,863 --> 00:00:12,445
ball B straight off the edge

6
00:00:12,947 --> 00:00:14,088
at two metres per second,

7
00:00:14,730 --> 00:00:16,973
and at the same instant it lets ball

8
00:00:16,994 --> 00:00:17,836
A simply drop.

9
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:20,207
Which one hits the floor first?

10
00:00:21,095 --> 00:00:21,477
Have a think.

11
00:00:25,186 --> 00:00:25,777
Let's release it.

12
00:00:26,483 --> 00:00:28,351
They hit the floor at the same instant.

13
00:00:29,114 --> 00:00:30,879
Here it is again in slow motion, with

14
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,444
a flash every tenth of a second.

15
00:00:33,067 --> 00:00:35,253
At every flash, the two balls are at

16
00:00:35,294 --> 00:00:36,457
exactly the same height.

17
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,808
B's sideways motion doesn't change its fall at

18
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:40,009
all.

19
00:00:40,956 --> 00:00:41,323
Why not?

20
00:00:41,995 --> 00:00:43,856
The answer gives us a way to analyse

21
00:00:44,157 --> 00:00:47,260
any projectile, as two motions sharing one clock.

22
00:00:48,088 --> 00:00:50,534
Once B leaves the rod, nothing pushes it

23
00:00:50,595 --> 00:00:50,956
forward.

24
00:00:51,558 --> 00:00:53,987
An object moving under gravity alone is called

25
00:00:54,008 --> 00:00:56,878
a projectile, and its path is its trajectory.

26
00:00:57,584 --> 00:00:58,553
We'll make two assumptions.

27
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:01,926
First, zero air resistance, the push of the

28
00:01:02,128 --> 00:01:03,270
air on a moving object.

29
00:01:03,892 --> 00:01:06,159
A feather fails that badly, but on the

30
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:08,466
Moon, with no air, a hammer and a

31
00:01:08,528 --> 00:01:10,453
feather dropped together landed together.

32
00:01:11,095 --> 00:01:12,719
For a steel ball over a short drop,

33
00:01:13,121 --> 00:01:14,924
air resistance is small enough to ignore.

34
00:01:15,787 --> 00:01:19,292
Second, gravity's acceleration is constant, nine point eight

35
00:01:19,353 --> 00:01:21,576
metres per second squared, downwards.

36
00:01:22,189 --> 00:01:23,864
Start with the horizontal direction.

37
00:01:24,549 --> 00:01:27,136
What forces act sideways on the ball in

38
00:01:27,157 --> 00:01:27,497
the air?

39
00:01:28,363 --> 00:01:29,432
With no air resistance,

40
00:01:30,018 --> 00:01:30,199
none.

41
00:01:30,844 --> 00:01:32,390
Its weight acts straight down.

42
00:01:33,235 --> 00:01:36,760
No horizontal force means no horizontal acceleration, so

43
00:01:36,821 --> 00:01:39,706
by Newton's first law, the horizontal velocity can't

44
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:40,167
change.

45
00:01:40,928 --> 00:01:43,469
B keeps moving sideways at two metres per

46
00:01:43,510 --> 00:01:46,231
second for the whole flight, the same twenty

47
00:01:46,292 --> 00:01:48,453
centimetres between every pair of flashes.

48
00:01:49,294 --> 00:01:53,545
So horizontal displacement is horizontal velocity times time.

49
00:01:54,269 --> 00:01:55,654
Now the vertical direction.

50
00:01:56,399 --> 00:01:58,947
The only force is the ball's weight, so

51
00:01:59,129 --> 00:02:01,837
the vertical acceleration is g, straight down.

52
00:02:02,761 --> 00:02:04,085
Taking up as positive,

53
00:02:04,628 --> 00:02:07,578
a y is minus nine point eight metres

54
00:02:07,639 --> 00:02:08,481
per second squared.

55
00:02:09,345 --> 00:02:12,576
B was launched horizontally, so its initial vertical

56
00:02:12,617 --> 00:02:14,684
velocity is zero, just like A's.

57
00:02:15,669 --> 00:02:19,801
Same starting vertical velocity, same vertical acceleration, so

58
00:02:20,143 --> 00:02:22,369
their vertical motions are identical.

59
00:02:23,253 --> 00:02:25,856
Every tenth of a second, each ball gains

60
00:02:26,037 --> 00:02:28,720
another zero point nine eight metres per second

61
00:02:28,841 --> 00:02:29,321
downwards,

62
00:02:29,843 --> 00:02:32,305
so the gaps between flashes keep growing.

63
00:02:32,914 --> 00:02:36,135
So one projectile becomes two simpler motions:

64
00:02:36,857 --> 00:02:41,019
constant velocity sideways, and constant acceleration vertically.

65
00:02:41,787 --> 00:02:42,759
Neither changes the other.

66
00:02:43,422 --> 00:02:46,068
Launching B faster doesn't hold it up, and

67
00:02:46,189 --> 00:02:48,053
falling doesn't slow it sideways.

68
00:02:48,716 --> 00:02:50,583
But both happen to one ball at the

69
00:02:50,644 --> 00:02:53,296
same moments, so they share one clock.

70
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:56,047
Time is the only thing the two columns

71
00:02:56,088 --> 00:02:56,630
have in common.

72
00:02:57,174 --> 00:02:58,139
Find it from one motion,

73
00:02:58,664 --> 00:02:59,851
and you can use it in the other.

74
00:03:00,495 --> 00:03:02,320
Let's use that link to find where B

75
00:03:02,382 --> 00:03:02,702
lands.

76
00:03:03,345 --> 00:03:05,655
We know how far B falls, but not

77
00:03:05,696 --> 00:03:08,086
for how long, so start with the vertical

78
00:03:08,127 --> 00:03:08,407
column.

79
00:03:09,229 --> 00:03:12,190
Up is positive, so B's vertical displacement is

80
00:03:12,311 --> 00:03:13,631
minus one point two metres,

81
00:03:14,252 --> 00:03:16,192
its initial vertical velocity is zero,

82
00:03:16,714 --> 00:03:18,794
and a y is minus nine point eight.

83
00:03:19,575 --> 00:03:22,116
Using s equals u t plus a half

84
00:03:22,197 --> 00:03:24,998
a t squared, minus one point two equals

85
00:03:25,139 --> 00:03:26,839
minus four point nine t squared.

86
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:29,524
So t squared is one point two over

87
00:03:29,585 --> 00:03:32,170
four point nine, and t is about zero

88
00:03:32,211 --> 00:03:33,372
point four nine seconds.

89
00:03:34,109 --> 00:03:36,623
Now carry that time across to the horizontal

90
00:03:36,664 --> 00:03:36,965
column.

91
00:03:37,649 --> 00:03:39,812
s x equals u x times t,

92
00:03:40,374 --> 00:03:42,898
two metres per second times zero point four

93
00:03:42,939 --> 00:03:45,142
nine five seconds, which is about zero point

94
00:03:45,223 --> 00:03:45,984
nine nine metres.

95
00:03:46,847 --> 00:03:47,452
Here's a surprise.

96
00:03:47,978 --> 00:03:50,455
Launch B at four metres per second instead.

97
00:03:50,999 --> 00:03:53,350
Its fall time doesn't change at all, so

98
00:03:53,391 --> 00:03:55,299
it simply lands twice as far out.

99
00:03:55,982 --> 00:03:58,363
The vertical motion sets the time, and the

100
00:03:58,404 --> 00:04:01,126
horizontal motion turns that time into a distance.

101
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:04,308
At any instant, though, the ball has just

102
00:04:04,529 --> 00:04:07,330
one velocity: the two components added tip to

103
00:04:07,371 --> 00:04:07,710
tail,

104
00:04:08,272 --> 00:04:11,313
a constant horizontal arrow plus a vertical arrow

105
00:04:11,394 --> 00:04:12,754
that keeps growing downwards.

106
00:04:13,436 --> 00:04:16,206
So as B falls, it speeds up, and

107
00:04:16,247 --> 00:04:19,037
its velocity turns steadily towards straight down.

108
00:04:19,722 --> 00:04:21,375
The path curves for the same reason.

109
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:24,741
Horizontal distance grows in step with time, but

110
00:04:24,782 --> 00:04:27,944
the drop grows with time squared, so the

111
00:04:27,985 --> 00:04:30,646
curve bends more and more, into a parabola.

112
00:04:31,278 --> 00:04:32,209
Here's a common mistake.

113
00:04:32,876 --> 00:04:35,646
Near the end, B is heading steeply down,

114
00:04:36,088 --> 00:04:38,015
so it looks as if it has slowed

115
00:04:38,116 --> 00:04:38,617
sideways.

116
00:04:39,301 --> 00:04:39,805
It hasn't.

117
00:04:40,471 --> 00:04:42,698
Its horizontal arrow is the same length at

118
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:44,124
landing as at launch.

119
00:04:44,809 --> 00:04:46,521
Only the vertical arrow has grown.

120
00:04:47,407 --> 00:04:49,548
A real ball does lose a little sideways

121
00:04:49,609 --> 00:04:52,070
speed, because air resistance pushes back on it,

122
00:04:52,612 --> 00:04:54,853
and that's exactly what our model leaves out.

123
00:04:55,628 --> 00:04:57,152
Here's a way to hold it together:

124
00:04:57,895 --> 00:04:58,475
two columns,

125
00:04:59,118 --> 00:04:59,559
one clock.

126
00:05:00,542 --> 00:05:03,264
Every horizontal quantity goes in one column, and

127
00:05:03,385 --> 00:05:05,046
every vertical quantity in the other.

128
00:05:05,709 --> 00:05:07,675
The only thing that crosses between them is

129
00:05:07,756 --> 00:05:08,096
time.

130
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,261
When you're stuck on a projectile,

131
00:05:10,882 --> 00:05:12,924
ask which column can give you the time,

132
00:05:13,445 --> 00:05:14,366
then carry it across.

133
00:05:17,082 --> 00:05:17,545
Your turn.

134
00:05:18,431 --> 00:05:21,219
Two balls start one point two five metres

135
00:05:21,399 --> 00:05:23,465
above level ground at the same instant.

136
00:05:24,046 --> 00:05:26,392
Ball A is dropped, and ball B is

137
00:05:26,432 --> 00:05:29,139
launched horizontally at three point zero metres per

138
00:05:29,159 --> 00:05:29,440
second.

139
00:05:30,262 --> 00:05:33,307
Neglecting air resistance, calculate the time each ball

140
00:05:33,367 --> 00:05:34,189
takes to land,

141
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:37,014
and how far ball B lands from the

142
00:05:37,054 --> 00:05:38,577
point directly below its start.

143
00:05:39,322 --> 00:05:40,295
Pause here to answer.

144
00:05:50,927 --> 00:05:54,089
Both balls start with zero vertical velocity and

145
00:05:54,170 --> 00:05:55,910
fall one point two five metres,

146
00:05:56,471 --> 00:05:58,912
so minus one point two five equals minus

147
00:05:58,973 --> 00:06:01,534
four point nine t squared, and t is

148
00:06:01,575 --> 00:06:03,936
about zero point five one seconds for each.

149
00:06:04,759 --> 00:06:07,024
B's horizontal distance is three point zero times

150
00:06:07,065 --> 00:06:09,189
that time, about one point five metres.

151
00:06:09,833 --> 00:06:11,963
The classic slip is giving B a longer

152
00:06:12,024 --> 00:06:13,491
time because it travels further.

153
00:06:14,115 --> 00:06:16,284
Its sideways motion doesn't change its fall.

154
00:06:18,017 --> 00:06:18,500
Last one.

155
00:06:19,066 --> 00:06:21,274
A ball is launched horizontally from the top

156
00:06:21,295 --> 00:06:21,756
of a cliff.

157
00:06:22,359 --> 00:06:24,202
Near the end of its flight, it's travelling

158
00:06:24,303 --> 00:06:27,750
steeply downwards, so a student claims its horizontal

159
00:06:27,791 --> 00:06:29,474
velocity must have decreased.

160
00:06:30,296 --> 00:06:32,658
Using the forces on the ball, explain whether

161
00:06:32,699 --> 00:06:33,638
the claim is correct

162
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:35,761
when air resistance is neglected.

163
00:06:36,504 --> 00:06:37,349
Pause here to answer.

164
00:06:48,123 --> 00:06:49,759
The claim is wrong in this model.

165
00:06:50,282 --> 00:06:52,264
The only force is the ball's weight, which

166
00:06:52,424 --> 00:06:53,445
acts straight down,

167
00:06:53,985 --> 00:06:56,507
so there's no horizontal force and no horizontal

168
00:06:56,547 --> 00:06:57,188
acceleration.

169
00:06:57,889 --> 00:07:00,428
The horizontal velocity keeps its launch value.

170
00:07:01,051 --> 00:07:03,738
The path is steeper because the vertical component

171
00:07:03,758 --> 00:07:06,405
has grown, not because the horizontal one shrank.

172
00:07:07,188 --> 00:07:10,037
Saying gravity slows it sideways is the slip

173
00:07:10,058 --> 00:07:10,498
to avoid.

174
00:07:12,390 --> 00:07:15,045
So a projectile is two motions with one

175
00:07:15,126 --> 00:07:15,427
clock.

176
00:07:16,233 --> 00:07:18,708
Ball B started with its velocity pointing straight

177
00:07:18,749 --> 00:07:19,271
sideways.

178
00:07:19,715 --> 00:07:21,804
But what if the launcher points upwards at

179
00:07:21,845 --> 00:07:22,226
an angle?

180
00:07:22,909 --> 00:07:25,093
Then the launch velocity has a horizontal part

181
00:07:25,575 --> 00:07:26,617
and a vertical part,

182
00:07:27,219 --> 00:07:29,022
and next time we'll see how the angle

183
00:07:29,124 --> 00:07:30,005
splits it into two.

184
00:07:32,995 --> 00:07:33,817
Here are the key notes.

185
00:07:34,341 --> 00:07:35,888
Pause the video if you need more time

